Z||000000||GT-maniac||09-11-2000||10:38 PM||||Hi folks I was just wondering what kind of costs people have had to pay out to replace <BR>an engine in a fiero with a stock replacement motor. I'm planning on swapping out my dead V6 with another used 2.8 V6. Would it be worthwhile to disconnect everything except the motor mounts and seperating the motor from the tranny, to save labor costs ?||||reg||1
Z||000001||fierocrazy||09-12-2000||11:20 AM||||i dont think it matters since they will prolly drop the whole cradle, i have heard of pulling it from the top, looks difficault to me<P>85GT 3.2||||reg||1
Z||000002||BellaFiero||09-12-2000||12:10 PM||||Looking to thow in a stock, but new, Duke into the Fiero... any idea how much I should accept as a legit price? Autozone wants $1640 for a brand new engine... good deal?? Me and my old man are shade tree mechanic pros, but Bella Donna, she's my daily driver... How long does it usually take a semi-skilled mechanic to throw a new engine into one of these bad boys? The engine is okay, but she's just turned 150,000 miles... on the lookout. Rather replace it now than on a stretch of highway   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by BellaFiero (edited 09-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||09-12-2000||12:22 PM||||When I pull motors I usually pull them with the tranny & starter on. It's a hell of allot easier. In fiero's case you can get the motor out the top but I don't see where it would save much time. I don't know if there's room to pull the motor and tranny out the top at the same time with a V6. <P>Autozone? New engine? more likely a rebuild. Definatly double check that. I think the only place you can get a truely new 4 cyl motor is GM. (The other crate people aren't interested in these.)<P>You can get another used motor for a couple hundred if you dig around. Depends what year yours is how hard they will be to find.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||BellaFiero||09-12-2000||12:34 PM||||Ogre, good call. Checked on that and the storage manager profusely apologized. I guess the sales guy didn't 'phrase it correctly.' Yep, it is a rebuilt engine for 1600 and change. I have a 1988 Coupe, want to put a stock 4 cyl. engine it ( incidentally - what's the difference between 4-tech and Duke. - ahem, besides the sticker - ). Any ideas on how to give the 4 a little more power, easy or not-so-easy bolt ons, etc. The question I really have is how much would a shop charge me to put it in. Oh, I know how much a few shops WANT, but how much should I ACCEPT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Let me know please!!<P>------------------<BR>"No, no... you can't put that there, it'll be in the way of the Johnson Rod!"  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Linx||09-12-2000||02:08 PM||||I yanked my V-6 from the top- without the tranny.  It wasnt that difficult.  I rebuilt the engine myself...even having it rebored.  I havent done the numbers, but off the top of my head, I want to say it cost around $500.<P>Before I'd started, I called around and found that Jasper Engines had a rebuilt for $1650- and that included a kickass warrenty of 3yrs and lots of miles- i almost fainted when they told me...and I cant remember for sure now...it was like 75k.  Incredible warrenty.||||reg||1
Z||000006||BellaFiero||09-12-2000||02:19 PM||||Anyone have a number for Jasper's Engines... maybe a web site? Good price and better than the warranty from Autozone! Heck, same price AS Autozone!! ||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfiero||09-12-2000||03:42 PM||||You have to use an '87 or '88 duke ('88 preferred) in your '88 coupe.  In '87 they went to DIS and in '88 they improved the balance and put the oil filter inside the pan.  The older ('84-'86) dukes will not work at all unless you plan on rewiring the car.....even then why do that?  The '87-'88 dukes are much better than the older ones.  They drive better and generally last longer too.<P>I personally would just buy a low mileage used motor instead of buying a new one.....it's more cost effective in the long run. Unless you plan on keeping the car for many years to come you won't recover the amount invested into the motor.<P>If you do get a Jasper or Auto Zone motor make sure it truly is for an '88 coupe......I have a sneaky suspicion they are selling the older ones.  If they are in fact '88 motors find out if they replaced the oil pump......the '88 oil pump assembly is very expensive ($300?), so they may not mess with it.  <P>Good luck and let us know how things go.  ||||reg||1
